From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Subject: [freebsd-ports PATCH v9] net-mgmt/signoz: Observability platform for
 logs, metrics, and traces
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:03:53 +0000

SigNoz is an open source observability platform. It brings logs,
metrics, and traces together in one application, with dashboards,
alerts, exceptions, and service maps. It is native to OpenTelemetry and
stores its data in ClickHouse.

This is the Community Edition of the query service and web interface.
Telemetry reaches ClickHouse through the SigNoz distribution of the
OpenTelemetry Collector, which is packaged separately.

Upstream pins github.com/bytedance/sonic v1.14.1, which does not build
with Go 1.26 or later. The port vendors sonic v1.15.4 instead, with the
upstream fix for mmap(2) on FreeBSD, which no sonic release includes
yet.

WWW: https://signoz.io/
---
Notes for review:

Build: v8 was built on 093b3979a71a, the parent of the base commit
below, with Go 1.27.2 and STRICT_DEPENDS set. DISTDIR was read-only
and the network was cut after fetch. extract, patch, build, stage, and
check-plist all passed. The staged binary ran as an unprivileged user
for 30 seconds without a panic or a missing-template warning. v9
changes only how the patch phase finds the sonic tree. It was checked
with a clean extract and patch: the upstream commit applied to the
tuple, and loader/mmap_unix.go passes ^uintptr(0) as the fd.
portclippy, portfmt, and portlint are clean. Not checked: a v9
build, a real poudriere build, aarch64, and a run from rc.d (that
needs root).

Why sonic must be replaced: signoz v0.145.0 pins
github.com/bytedance/sonic v1.14.1. Its internal/rt package defines
GoMapIterator only for go1.24 && !go1.26, so with the tree's Go 1.26
or 1.27 it fails with "undefined: GoMapIterator". This is not specific
to FreeBSD, and go.mk offers no older Go. sonic added Go 1.26 support
in v1.15.0. signoz main and v0.146.0 still pin v1.14.1; SigNoz PR
#11975, "fix(deps): bump bytedance/sonic to v1.15.0 for Go 1.26
compatibility", has been open since July. Separately, sonic's loader
maps memory with MAP_ANON and fd 0, which FreeBSD rejects with EINVAL,
so the binary panics at startup. bytedance/sonic commit bf12ab8,
"MAP_ANON should be set to -1 , not required on Linux, required on
BSD. + go fmt", fixes that and is in no release yet.

How: go.mk vendors the modules at extract time, in go-post-extract, so
a patch to go.mod in files/ comes too late (Porter's Handbook, section
6.6.8, "Overriding go.mod in Modules Mode"). The tree's common answer
for a GO_MODULE port that needs a newer copy of one module is to fetch
it with GH_TUPLE and swap it into vendor/ in pre-patch; 22 GO_MODULE
ports do this, e.g. security/nebula, databases/imposm3, net/slackcat,
and www/oneshot. The tuple is the sonic v1.15.4 release tag. Its go.mod
differs from v1.14.1's only in the loader version, so it needs no new
modules. go.mod and vendor/modules.txt still name v1.14.1 and v0.3.0,
so go's vendor consistency check passes; "go version -m" reports
those versions, as it does for nebula's x/ modules.

The mmap fix is PATCHFILES from the upstream commit. Its paths are
relative to the sonic repository. bsd.port.mk has one PATCH_WRKSRC for
every patch, distribution or local, and a PATCHFILES entry may set only
its strip level (patch[:-pX][:group]). The distribution patches alone
run with PATCH_DIST_ARGS (Mk/Scripts/do-patch.sh), so the port adds
-d ${WRKSRC_sonic} there and keeps the default's other flags.
PATCH_WRKSRC stays at WRKSRC, so a later files/patch-* applies to
signoz as usual. net/norm does the same for an upstream commit to its
protolib GH_TUPLE, PATCH_DIST_ARGS= -d ../protolib-702e909, and
editors/nvi-m17n sets -d for its distribution patch as well. The
commit touches only loader/mmap_unix.go and applies cleanly to
v1.15.4. When a sonic release carries the fix, the tuple becomes that
tag and PATCH_SITES, PATCHFILES, and PATCH_DIST_ARGS go.

Configuration: upstream's conf/example.yaml is not the defaults and
cannot be installed as the active config. Started with it as is,
signoz exits before startup on "sharder.single.org_id invalid uuid
org_id", "user.root.email invalid email", and an external_url of
"<unset>". With those fixed it still changes behavior against the
built-in defaults: alertmanager group_wait 1m (default 30s),
group_interval 1m (5m) and repeat_interval 1h (4h), span metrics on,
password self-reset allowed, service-account analytics on, a 64 MiB
cache (128 MiB), cloud agent v0.0.8 (v0.0.15), and a timeout
excluded_routes list missing /api/v1/export_raw_data. Its header says
"Do not modify this file". It stays in DATADIR as the reference to
every key.

The port installs its own short sample instead, as
/usr/local/etc/signoz/signoz.yaml.sample. It sets only the ClickHouse
DSN, to its default, and points at example.yaml. signoz has no default
config path: it reads each --config file in order, later ones
overriding earlier ones key by key, then SIGNOZ_* variables. rc.d now
defaults signoz_config to /usr/local/etc/signoz/signoz.yaml and takes
a space-separated list, so local overrides can live in more files
beside it. signoz_config="" still runs on built-in defaults and
signoz_env.

No other packaging was found to follow. AUR, nixpkgs, Homebrew, and
Repology have no signoz. Upstream's Docker image runs from /root with
no config file, and its systemd example (SigNoz/foundry) unpacks the
release under /opt/signoz and sets paths by environment variable.

Templates: the three .gotmpl files are runtime configuration, not
data. The email store reads invitation.gotmpl and reset_password.gotmpl
from emailing.templates.directory; a missing file only logs a warning,
and that email then fails to send. alertmanager.signoz.templates is a
list of globs whose definitions are parsed in order, so a later file
can redefine email.signoz.html. Neither set is embedded in the binary.
They move to /usr/local/etc/signoz/templates as @sample files, as
sysutils/munin-master does with its templates, so local edits survive
upgrades. The compiled-in defaults point there.

rc.d: the script follows net-mgmt/prometheus3's. rc.subr runs the
command as ${name}_user through su -m, so daemon(8) runs as signoz and
cannot create its pidfile in /var/run. start_precmd runs as root
first and creates it owned by signoz. 191 rc scripts in the tree
create the pidfile this way, 129 of them around daemon(8), e.g.
net-mgmt/prometheus3, net-mgmt/pushgateway, and
net-mgmt/mysqld_exporter.

The other workarounds are the prebuilt :web frontend (as www/grafana
does), the post-patch REINPLACE_CMD for default paths, TMPPLIST for
the hashed web file names, USERS and GROUPS (UID 485), and
ONLY_FOR_ARCHS.

Changes in v9:
- Point only the distribution patch at the sonic tuple, with -d in
  PATCH_DIST_ARGS, instead of setting PATCH_WRKSRC for the whole port.
- Notes: say where the rc.d pidfile precmd comes from and why it is
  needed.
- Link to v8: id:f747bbebcaeb26db6bcccbb1b818b773374e3d80.1792086375.git.claude@p7x.dev

